<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Analysis of Correlation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation results on our private test set are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 3.5 Analysis of Correlation Metrics ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Large Multimodal Models for Automatic Pronunciation Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The PCC values are approximately 0.9, indicating a strong linear correlation. In contrast, the SCC values are around 0.6 across sentence-level metrics such as accuracy, fluency, prosody, and total score. This discrepancy suggests that SCC may serve as a more suitable evaluation metric for APA tasks as model quality improves, since PCC assumes a linear correlation and requires data to be continuous and approximately Gaussian distributed, whereas SCC only assumes ordinal relationships.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation results on the private test set</span></figcaption>
